<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guru Gobind Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1666–1708</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guru Gobind Singh was the tenth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> Guru, who in 1699 founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a>, the order of initiated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhs">Sikhs</a>. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> hill chiefs of the Sivaliks, including Bilaspur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> at Bhangani in 1688, and Mughal forces. His two youngest sons were executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirhind">Sirhind</a> in 1704, an act the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malerkotla">Malerkotla</a> protested.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh.jpg" alt="Guru Gobind Singh" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painting_of_Guru_Gobind_Singh,_found_within_the_Anandpuri_Hazuri_Bir_codex_of_the_Dasam_Granth,_ca.1690%27s%E2%80%931700%27s_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
